---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'Turf — Get started: add the Turf MCP server to your agent';
const description = 'Install Turf with Homebrew or one script, connect it to Claude Code, Cursor, Codex or any MCP client in one command, and deploy a first stack from your agent.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <section class="subhero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Get started · Turf 1.0</p>
      <h1>Install Turf and connect it to your agent.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Homebrew installs the Turf™ MCP server and its engine. One command registers it with
        Claude Code, Claude Desktop, Cursor, Codex, Gemini CLI or VS Code. In Claude Code and Claude
        Desktop, Turf runs on your Claude subscription — no model API key.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>1 · Install</h2>
      <p>The tap installs four binaries: <code>turf-mcp-server</code>, the MCP server your agent talks
        to; the engine behind it, <code>turf-engine</code> and <code>turf-builtin-provider</code>, which
        runs as a service so a rollout keeps going when the conversation that started it ends; and the
        <code>turf</code> CLI.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Homebrew · macOS &amp; Linux</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> brew install restatedev/tap/restate-server turfbuild/tap/turf
<span class="ps1">$</span> brew services start turf
<span class="cm"># the engine, now and at every login</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> turf-mcp-server engine status
<span class="cm"># later: brew upgrade turf &amp;&amp; brew services restart turf</span></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>One script · with or without Homebrew</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> curl -fsSL https://get.turf.build/install.sh | bash</pre>
      </div>
      <p>With Homebrew on your PATH the script runs the same install and starts the service. Without it,
        the script unpacks the release into <code>~/.turf/bin</code>, and you run the engine yourself:
        <code>turf-mcp-server engine run</code>.</p>
      <p>Turf is free for noncommercial use under PolyForm Noncommercial 1.0.0. Companies can evaluate
        Turf free for 60 days and license it for production at
        <a href="/licensing/">turf.build/licensing</a>.</p>
      <p>Turf sends anonymous usage telemetry to Turf LLC by default: the version, the platform,
        counts of resources, changes and runs, and an anonymous installation id. It never sends
        resource contents, credentials, state, variable values or names. Turn it off with
        <code>turf telemetry off</code>, or with <code>TURF_TELEMETRY=off</code> or
        <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> in the engine's environment; the full field list and the endpoint
        are at <a href="/telemetry/">turf.build/telemetry</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="connect">2 · Connect your agent</h2>
      <p>One command registers Turf with every agent on this machine. It finds the ones you have
        installed, lets you pick from the list, and writes each config with the server's absolute path,
        so an app launched from the Dock finds it too. It needs Node 18+ and uses
        <a href="https://github.com/neon-solutions/add-mcp">add-mcp</a>, Neon's open-source installer;
        running it again replaces the entry.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Register the server</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> npx add-mcp@2 <span class="str">"$(command -v turf-mcp-server)"</span> --name turf -g</pre>
      </div>
      <p>In Claude Code and Claude Desktop, Turf runs on your Claude subscription — no model API key.
        Restart Claude Desktop or Cursor after the config changes.</p>

      <h3>Without Node: your client's own command</h3>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Register the server</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># Claude Code</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> claude mcp add turf --scope user -- turf-mcp-server
<span class="cm"># Codex CLI</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> codex mcp add turf -- turf-mcp-server
<span class="cm"># Gemini CLI</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> gemini mcp add -s user turf turf-mcp-server
<span class="cm"># VS Code</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> code --add-mcp <span class="str">'&#123;"name":"turf","command":"turf-mcp-server"&#125;'</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>Claude Desktop and Cursor read the same block from their config file. Use the path
        <code>command -v turf-mcp-server</code> prints, then restart the app:</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>claude_desktop_config.json · ~/.cursor/mcp.json</span></div>
<pre>&#123;
  <span class="str">"mcpServers"</span>: &#123;
    <span class="str">"turf"</span>: &#123;
      <span class="str">"command"</span>: <span class="str">"/path/to/turf-mcp-server"</span>
    &#125;
  &#125;
&#125;</pre>
      </div>
      <p>The server speaks MCP over stdio by default, or over HTTP with
        <code>turf-mcp-server --transport http</code> (binds loopback).</p>

      <h2>3 · Deploy from your agent</h2>
      <p>Turf's prompts are slash commands in Claude Code. Start with the walkthrough: it runs a real
        example from <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples">turf-examples</a> and shows
        the approval gate on the way.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>In Claude Code</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># first-run walkthrough</span>
<span class="ps1">&gt;</span> /turf:guide
<span class="cm"># deploy or update a configuration directory</span>
<span class="ps1">&gt;</span> /turf:up path/to/config
<span class="cm"># tear it down</span>
<span class="ps1">&gt;</span> /turf:destroy path/to/config</pre>
      </div>
      <p>In any other client, ask in words: “deploy the configuration in ./infra with Turf”.
        Slash-command arguments are one word each, so the path has no spaces. Every change is planned,
        shown to you, and applied as approved, round by round — with an approval gate in between. Nothing runs
        off-plan.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Prefer a standalone agent? The Turf CLI</h2>
      <p>Turf's own agent, <code>turf chat</code>, drives the same server and engine with a model you
        bring. Set a key for a hosted model, or run fully local with no key and no cost.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Choose a model</span></div>
<pre><span class="cm"># hosted frontier model</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=<span class="str">sk-...</span>

<span class="cm"># or a local model — no API key, no cost</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> turf --model <span class="str">dmr/ai/qwen3</span> chat

<span class="cm"># or a private GPU endpoint (vLLM, LM Studio, gateways)</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> turf --base-url <span class="str">https://gpu.internal/v1</span> --model <span class="str">my-model</span> chat</pre>
      </div>
      <p>Chat interactively, or converge a whole stack from a config directory.</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>The Turf CLI</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> turf chat
<span class="ps1">$</span> cd path/to/config &amp;&amp; turf up
<span class="ps1">$</span> cd path/to/config &amp;&amp; turf destroy</pre>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Container image &amp; supply-chain verification</h2>
      <p>A multi-arch image is published to GHCR and signed with cosign (keyless, via GitHub OIDC).</p>
      <div class="codeblock">
        <div class="cb-head"><span>Pull &amp; verify</span></div>
<pre><span class="ps1">$</span> docker pull ghcr.io/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server:latest

<span class="cm"># verify the signature</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> cosign verify ghcr.io/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server:latest \
    --certificate-identity-regexp <span class="str">'^https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server/'</span> \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer <span class="str">https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com</span>

<span class="cm"># verify the SBOM attestation</span>
<span class="ps1">$</span> cosign verify-attestation --type cyclonedx ghcr.io/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server:latest \
    --certificate-identity-regexp <span class="str">'^https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-mcp-server/'</span> \
    --certificate-oidc-issuer <span class="str">https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p>Installed with Homebrew? Verify the binary's provenance with
        <code>gh attestation verify turf --repo turfbuild/turf</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>See what Turf supports today on the <a href="/compatibility/">Compatibility</a> page.</li>
        <li>Browse runnable stacks in <a href="https://github.com/turfbuild/turf-examples">turf-examples</a>.</li>
        <li>Building a GPU cloud? Read <a href="/reference-architectures/">Metal to Tokens</a>, the
          reference architecture.</li>
        <li>Evaluating Turf at a company? <a href="/licensing/#pilot">Start a free 60-day pilot</a>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
